// A `foreach` variable may not take a const generic parameter's name. This file compiled CLEAN and
// returned 48 — `16 + 16 + 16`, the const param summed three times — rather than the 6 the loop says.
//
// Unlike the local spelling, `foreach` and `match` binders are exempt from kama's general shadowing
// ban, and for an ORDINARY name that exemption is harmless: the inner binding wins, which is what the
// author wrote. It is only a const generic parameter that gets discarded instead of shadowed, because
// `emitExpression` resolves one ahead of every runtime name. So this rule is about const params, not
// about widening that ban.
fn int32 f<const F: int32>() {
    InlineArray<int32, 3> xs = [1, 2, 3];
    int32 t = 0;
    foreach (int32 F in xs) { t = t + F; }   // ERROR: the loop variable shadows the const generic param `F`
    return t;
}
fn int32 main() { return f::<16>(); }
